1104 #edb/ref 17592186348696 13194139535429 true] [17592186348691 1110 #uuid "372af865-2473-5848-8737-a2f5490bd34d" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348692 1034 "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348692 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535429 true] [17592186348692 1111 #uuid "ddf23edb-1c7e-5a7a-a63f-18ad36e72c44" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348693 1034 "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-2y" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348693 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535429 true] [17592186348693 1111 #uuid "3e53b892-e209-5b0a-b009-a87684ba7a2a" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348694 1034 "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+2{y}^{2}" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348694 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535429 true] [17592186348694 1111 #uuid "a92aeb6b-7acc-5a15-b47e-ca5c67da5706" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348695 1034 "y(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+1)" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348695 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535429 true] [17592186348695 1111 #uuid "e284c632-b7ce-58b4-9355-7b884ffe9af1" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348696 1034 "y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-2)" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348696 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535429 true] [17592186348696 1111 #uuid "f15473f2-f7f7-5be0-ad13-9030cf119470" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348697 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348698 13194139535429 true] [17592186348697 1089 "q-131348" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348697 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535429 true] [17592186348697 1100 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=({x}^{2}+xy)\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0).$" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348697 1113 #uuid "5a0fbb73-17a7-5477-a476-848bd5a49fd1" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348697 1145 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+y}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(1/2,0)} & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2}+0)}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348699 13194139535429 true] [17592186348698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348700 13194139535429 true] [17592186348698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348701 13194139535429 true] [17592186348698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348702 13194139535429 true] [17592186348698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348703 13194139535429 true] [17592186348698 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535429 true] [17592186348698 1041 "selection" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348698 1104 #edb/ref 17592186348702 13194139535429 true] [17592186348698 1110 #uuid "a20ed83d-6dbb-5df3-8d63-7e1abce79b7f" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348699 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348699 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535429 true] [17592186348699 1111 #uuid "aa529fc2-f6f0-5885-a71e-3f511a4aad6c" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348700 1034 "2" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348700 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535429 true] [17592186348700 1111 #uuid "065cedf5-9bab-593c-984c-3ce8c5e1b3e1" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348701 1034 "1" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348701 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535429 true] [17592186348701 1111 #uuid "2ab7f801-0d22-5057-9260-3f04b859cf34" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348702 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348702 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535429 true] [17592186348702 1111 #uuid "a86d0f20-45be-5a83-b04c-12f29d22abd6" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348703 1034 "0" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348703 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535429 true] [17592186348703 1111 #uuid "47f8816b-53dc-54d6-9e6f-521e42be3795" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348704 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348705 13194139535429 true] [17592186348704 1089 "q-131361" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348704 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535429 true] [17592186348704 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064672/assets/1709a94b7361a1e8764d94199f9e84802ae925a09e6481250d0846b83bb6549f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction.\n- $F$ is irrotational" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348704 1113 #uuid "ecf0697a-f0a9-5859-b0bb-dbd418346e6a" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348704 1145 "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064672/assets/7c831425eae7afd709d546a8e05100f495adc04bba6450cfa56d370b415a28f5.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate clockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point into the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points into from the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348706 13194139535429 true] [17592186348705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348707 13194139535429 true] [17592186348705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348708 13194139535429 true] [17592186348705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348709 13194139535429 true] [17592186348705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348710 13194139535429 true] [17592186348705 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535429 true] [17592186348705 1041 "selection" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348705 1104 #edb/ref 17592186348707 13194139535429 true] [17592186348705 1110 #uuid "74dedc58-2d4d-5b56-bbd7-6b41ec1aa502" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348706 1034 "I and IV only" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348706 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535429 true] [17592186348706 1111 #uuid "5759cd00-873a-5339-9098-3f3c870f3f7a" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348707 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348707 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535429 true] [17592186348707 1111 #uuid "96290768-da07-5255-901f-28473f135ada" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348708 1034 "II and IV only" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348708 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535429 true] [17592186348708 1111 #uuid "5bc277bc-0b07-522a-bad0-6bf3464db126" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348709 1034 "I only" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348709 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535429 true] [17592186348709 1111 #uuid "e2495c6f-dfd1-5308-85aa-74ada0f29969" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348710 1034 "II only" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348710 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535429 true] [17592186348710 1111 #uuid "9861f9e0-ee22-5715-8db2-36be0666daac" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348711 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348712 13194139535429 true] [17592186348711 1089 "q-131366" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348711 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535429 true] [17592186348711 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064672/assets/781a4e33b8b55762b2dcb7575d08315b5842f83d6d78a5136a1c353eefd3069d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." 13194139535429 true] [17592186348711 1113 #uuid "2dcaf3bb-a51b-51ee-bef2-7f7484d8f502" 13194139535429 true] [17592186348711 1145 "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064672/assets/979b63f21e56924bd7880f9487cf829089e38cf14d287e70196c7a661e41f97f.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would n